From 3b01160e48c6c74fa85594e22a4879dceb6a3cee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 00:13:21 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 05/18] [bigint] emit inlineable acquire and release glue for
 foreign handles

`reussir_rt::rcgmp::Mpz`, the owned `Nat`/`Int` handle of the polymorphic
FFI, retains and releases through the rcgmp runtime's C entry points, so
every clone and drop of an integer in a Rust container is a call, also
for an immediate, where retain and release do nothing.

`Mpz`'s `Clone`/`Drop` now call `__reussir_rcgmp_ffi_acquire` and
`__reussir_rcgmp_ffi_release`, glue the compiler emits as `rcgmp.retain`
and `rcgmp.release`. Rendering `Mpz` records a glue entry next to the
shared-record wrappers' (it declares nothing in the texture), so every
program with a texture that spells the handle gets the pair, also when
the integer is only a type argument inside a foreign body.
Monomorphization also adds the pair to every other program that uses Nat
or Int (`uses_bigint`, the condition under which rrc links the rcgmp
runtime), for runtime code outside the textures that holds an `Mpz`.
Codegen defines the pair `weak_odr` in every codegen unit, so the
rcgmp lowering decides the immediate test (the dialect does not make its
tag an ABI) and LLVM inlines the glue into each unit's textures wherever
their target attributes allow it.

The glue has one fixed name, defined alike by every module, so each
linked image must bind its own copy, next to its own rcgmp runtime: the
unit lists it in the module attribute `reussir.hidden_symbols`, and
`qualifyFfiSymbols` hides the listed definitions once the module is
translated, in rrc and in reussir-translate pipelines alike. It stays out
of the bookkeeping for foreign releases (no `reussir.foreign_drop`, not
an FFI symbol, not among a texture's release bindings): `rcgmp.release` is a leaf.

`Mpz`'s methods stay `#[inline]`, so reussir-rt itself references neither
the glue nor the rcgmp runtime, and a program without integers links
without them.

Measured on aarch64 at -Oaggressive, textures compiled for the program's
CPU: 100 copy-on-write copies of a 1000-element `Vec<Nat>` called
`rcgmp_mpz_retain` 100,000 times and `rcgmp_mpz_release` 101,000 times,
for immediates and heap values alike, and call neither now; the loops
test the low bit. The object's text grows from 4215 to 4779 bytes (13%).
With four codegen units and the program compiled for the textures'
target (`--target-cpu generic --target-features=`), a program of integer
containers keeps 3 call relocations to the glue, as with one unit; a glue
defined only in its home unit left 21.

Tests: frontend/bigint_ffi_glue.rr (the glue's MLIR and module attribute,
its MIR round trip, hidden weak_odr definitions in a program without
textures and in each of two codegen units, none in a program without
integers, no call to the glue or to rcgmp's retain/release in the -O
loops; the program runs), codegen/hidden_symbols.mlir (the attribute
through reussir-translate), frontend/bigint_ffi_dynlib.rr (a shared
library exports none of the glue), frontend/bigint_ffi_phantom.rr (Nat
only as a type argument of a foreign body; links and runs),
sanitizer/bigint-ffi-generic-asan.rr.
